Title: SCENE 09 - THE CRASH
Credit: Written by
Author: Words To Film By
Draft date: January 2026
Notes: Racoon Rescue V2 - The House Party Crash

===

.THE CRASH - HOUSE PARTY

INT./EXT. DANNY'S TRUCK (CAREENING) - NIGHT

The windshield is a smear of BLOOD and FUR. Danny can't see.

The raccoon is ON HIS FACE. Claws dig into his scalp. That UNHOLY SCREAM fills the cab - part woman, part power tool, pure rage.

Danny throws an elbow. Misses. Hits the rearview mirror. Glass SHATTERS.

DANNY
GET OFF--

The wheel jerks LEFT. The truck JUMPS the curb.

Through the chaos, a GLIMPSE: houses with lights on. Music. A wooden fence decorated with PAPER LANTERNS.

The raccoon's teeth find Danny's bandaged ear. The existing wound.

Danny SCREAMS.

His hands leave the wheel entirely.


EXT. RESIDENTIAL STREET - OCEANVIEW ESTATES - CONTINUOUS

The truck rockets across a manicured lawn. Sprinkler heads EXPLODE under the tires.

A GARDEN GNOME goes flying.

The fence looms. White picket. Decorated with TIKI TORCHES and a banner:

"HAPPY 50TH MARGARET!"


EXT. GERALD'S BACKYARD - CONTINUOUS

PARTY IN FULL SWING.

Thirty people. String lights everywhere. A FOLDING TABLE set up for BEER PONG, surrounded by COLLEGE KIDS in backwards caps.

MARGARET (50) stands near a sheet cake, laughing with her book club.

A DJ booth. Music thumping. RED SOLO CUPS everywhere.

GERALD (58) emerges from the sliding door carrying a fresh bag of ice. He wears a Hawaiian shirt that was clearly Margaret's idea.

GIRL AT PONG TABLE
--okay but you have to BOUNCE it--

And then:

SOUND. Like the world tearing.

Everyone's head turns toward the fence.


EXT. GERALD'S FENCE - CONTINUOUS

The truck EXPLODES through the white picket barrier.

SLOW MOTION:

Fence posts CARTWHEEL through the air. Paper lanterns scatter like frightened birds. A tiki torch spirals, trailing fire.

The truck's headlights cut through the party smoke like the eyes of a demon.


EXT. GERALD'S BACKYARD - CONTINUOUS

NORMAL SPEED.

The truck PLOWS through the beer pong table.

RED SOLO CUPS launch into the air like confetti. Beer sprays in a golden arc. The ping pong balls scatter.

COLLEGE KIDS dive in every direction.

The truck finally STOPS - front bumper six inches from the kidney-shaped pool. Steam hisses from the hood. One headlight still working, casting a single beam across the water.

For THREE FULL SECONDS:

Silence.

Nobody moves.

The DJ's music keeps playing. Something upbeat. Obscene in context.

Every person in the backyard stares at the truck.


INT. DANNY'S TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

Danny sits behind the wheel.

His face is a HORROR SHOW. Blood streams from his reopened ear. Fresh scratches crosshatch his cheeks. His shirt is torn, revealing more wounds on his chest and shoulder.

He breathes. In. Out.

On the passenger seat, the raccoon sits perfectly still. They lock eyes.

Beat.

BANDIT scrambles up and over the headrest. Danny flinches, but the raccoon has somewhere to be. It squeezes through the shattered rear window and disappears into the night.

Danny watches it go.

He doesn't try to stop it.


EXT. GERALD'S BACKYARD - CONTINUOUS

The party-goers remain frozen. Then:

FRAT BRO #1
What the FUCK?

This breaks the spell.

FRAT BRO #2
(to anyone with a phone)
Are you recording? RECORD THIS!

PHONES come out. A dozen. More. The SCREENS light up like fireflies.

A GIRL starts screaming - delayed shock. Her friend tries to calm her.

Someone's DAD pulls people back from the truck.

Margaret stands by her ruined cake, mouth open, sheet of frosting splattered across her blouse.


ANGLE ON: The truck cab.

Danny is visible through the driver's window. The lone headlight illuminates him like a spotlight.

Blood. Scratches. Dress whites visible beneath his torn jacket.

THE THOUSAND-YARD STARE.

Around him, PHONE FLASHES pop. Multiple angles. Different heights. He is being recorded from every direction.

Danny just sits there. Doesn't move. Doesn't speak.

Done. Finished. A man who has finally hit bottom.


ANGLE ON: Gerald.

The bag of ice has DROPPED. Cubes scatter across the patio.

Gerald walks toward the destruction in a daze. He passes:
- The decimated beer pong table
- His shattered fence
- His wife's ruined birthday banner

He stops at the driver's side door.

Looks through the window at Danny.

Danny looks back.

GERALD
What in the--
(no words sufficient)
WHAT IS--

Danny's voice is barely audible. Hoarse. Defeated.

DANNY
...sorry.

Gerald blinks. Processes. His eyes drop to Danny's chest. To the torn jacket. To what's visible underneath:

The DRESS WHITE UNIFORM. Navy insignia.

Gerald's face changes. This isn't a random drunk. This is MILITARY.

He looks back at his destroyed backyard. At the phones still recording. At his wife, crying now.

GERALD
(to himself, almost wondering)
My wife left me for her tennis instructor.
(beat)
I just wanted one quiet year.

He stares at Danny. Danny stares back.

Neither man has anything else to say.


ANGLE ON: The tree line at the back of the property.

TWO GLOWING EYES watch from the darkness.

BANDIT sits in the branches. Unbothered. Grooming a paw.

He CHITTERS once - it sounds almost like laughter - and disappears into the night.


BACK TO: Danny in the truck.

In the distance, the first SIREN wails.

RED SOLO CUPS litter the destroyed lawn like spent shell casings.

The DJ finally kills the music. The silence is worse.

Danny closes his eyes.

DANNY (V.O.)
(barely a whisper)
It's fine.

He opens his eyes. Looks at the phones. At the destruction. At the life he's just ended.

DANNY (V.O.)
This is...

He can't finish it. For the first time, he can't say the words.

He just sits there.

Waiting.


EXT. GERALD'S BACKYARD - MOMENTS LATER

WIDER SHOT now. The full scope of destruction visible.

The truck sits at a crooked angle, one headlight still burning.

Party guests cluster in groups, phones still out.

Gerald stands next to Danny's door like a man who has no idea what to do next.

Margaret has been led inside by friends.

And Danny Vega, Petty Officer First Class, eleven years of service, remains motionless in the cab.

Not fighting it anymore.

The first RED AND BLUE LIGHTS appear on the street beyond the ruined fence.

He doesn't even look.


MATCH CUT TO:

The same defeated eyes.

Older now. Cleaned up. But the same.


INT. NAVY JAG COURTROOM - DAY

Danny on the witness stand. Present day.

The court waits for his answer.

Commander Wells holds the evidence bag with the raccoon paw print.

COMMANDER WELLS
Petty Officer?

Danny looks at the paw print.

He almost smiles. Almost.

DANNY
There was a party.

HARD CUT TO BLACK.

.END OF SCENE

===

**SCENE NOTES:**

PAGE COUNT: Approximately 2-2.5 pages

WOOD CHIPPER MOMENT: Gerald recognizing the military uniform - the realization that this disaster comes with PAPERWORK, with SYSTEMS, with bureaucracy beyond a simple police report.

POWER SHIFT:
- Scene opens: Danny vs. Raccoon (Raccoon dominant)
- Mid-scene: Danny vs. Witnesses (Total exposure)
- End: Danny vs. Himself (Surrender)

KEY VISUAL: Red solo cups scattered like confetti, the lone headlight, phones recording from every angle.

REFRAIN: "It's fine. This is..." - Danny cannot complete his signature phrase. The character break is complete.

GERALD'S LINE: "My wife left me for her tennis instructor. I just wanted one quiet year." - Thematic parallel. Collateral damage. Another man who thought he'd earned peace.

RACCOON EXIT: Bandit escapes unbothered. He was never the villain. He was the universe's correction.

TRANSITION: Match cut on Danny's eyes bridges back to the frame story courtroom, completing the flashback structure.
